push.vapidFlow:
All of this runs on raw Web Crypto in a Worker — no npm deps needed even for payload encryption, since Workers implement ECDSA/P-256, ECDH, HKDF and AES-GCM natively (developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/web-crypto/).

1. GENERATE THE VAPID KEYPAIR ONCE (a local script or a one-off admin route, not per-request):
   const kp = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({name:'ECDSA', namedCurve:'P-256'}, true, ['sign','verify']);
   const rawPub = new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.exportKey('raw', kp.publicKey)); // 65 bytes, starts 0x04
   const pkcs8Priv = new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.exportKey('pkcs8', kp.privateKey));
   Base64url-encode both. Public key becomes both the browser's applicationServerKey and the VAPID Authorization header's "k=" value. Store the private key as `wrangler secret put VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` (base64url PKCS8), re-imported at request time with crypto.subtle.importKey('pkcs8', bytes, {name:'ECDSA', namedCurve:'P-256'}, false, ['sign']).

2. BROWSER SUBSCRIBE FLOW (same API on iOS/Android; iOS just gates when it's callable — see gotchas):
   const reg = await navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js');
   const perm = await Notification.requestPermission(); // must run inside a user-gesture handler on iOS
   if (perm !== 'granted') return;
   const sub = await reg.pushManager.subscribe({
     userVisibleOnly: true,
     applicationServerKey: urlBase64ToUint8Array(VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY_B64URL),
   });
   await fetch('/api/subscribe', {method:'POST', body: JSON.stringify(sub)}); // sub.toJSON() = {endpoint, keys:{p256dh, auth}}
   Source for the full register/subscribe/urlBase64ToUint8Array pattern: developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Progressive_web_apps/Tutorials/js13kGames/Re-engageable_Notifications_Push

3. SIGN THE VAPID JWT IN THE WORKER (raw Web Crypto, ~25 lines, confirmed the ECDSA output format matches JWS directly):
   const header = {typ:'JWT', alg:'ES256'};
   const payload = {aud: new URL(sub.endpoint).origin, exp: Math.floor(Date.now()/1000)+12*3600, sub:'mailto:you@example.com'};
   const unsigned = b64url(JSON.stringify(header)) + '.' + b64url(JSON.stringify(payload));
   const sigBuf = await crypto.subtle.sign({name:'ECDSA', hash:'SHA-256'}, privateKey, new TextEncoder().encode(unsigned));
   const jwt = unsigned + '.' + b64url(new Uint8Array(sigBuf));
   Critically: MDN and the WebCrypto spec confirm crypto.subtle.sign for ECDSA returns raw IEEE P1363 (r||s concatenated, 64 bytes for P-256) — this is exactly what JWS ES256 requires, so there is NO DER-to-raw conversion step needed (that step trips up people porting from Node's crypto module, which does emit DER by default). Source: developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/SubtleCrypto/sign and RFC 7518 (JWS) ES256 format definition.
   exp MUST be <= 24h from now (RFC 8292 / rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8292.html — VAPID spec). aud MUST be exactly the origin (scheme+host) of the subscription's endpoint URL, not the full endpoint.

4. AUTHORIZATION HEADER + REQUEST:
   Authorization: `vapid t=${jwt}, k=${VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY_B64URL}`
   TTL: '<seconds>' (required by the push service, e.g. 2419200 for 4 weeks; use a short TTL like 300-3600 for time-sensitive reminders since a stale reminder delivered hours late is worse than dropped)
   Urgency: 'normal' (optional; very-low/low/normal/high)
   POST to sub.endpoint with no body for a "tickle" (empty push, cheapest and simplest), or an encrypted body per step 5 if you want the notification text delivered in the push itself.

5. PAYLOAD ENCRYPTION (RFC 8291, aes128gcm) — ONLY NEEDED IF YOU PUT TEXT IN THE PUSH ITSELF:
   - ECDH: generate an ephemeral P-256 keypair per message, crypto.subtle.deriveBits with the subscription's p256dh as the peer public key, to get the shared ECDH secret.
   - HKDF (all via crypto.subtle with the 'HKDF' algorithm, or manual HMAC-SHA-256 if you want single-call control): PRK_key = HMAC-SHA-256(auth_secret, ecdh_secret); combine with info = "WebPush: info" || 0x00 || ua_public(65B) || as_public(65B) to get IKM; then standard RFC 8188 HKDF-Expand with salt (16 random bytes) and info = "Content-Encoding: aes128gcm" || 0x00 to get the 16-byte CEK, and info = "Content-Encoding: nonce" || 0x00 to get the 12-byte nonce.
   - Wire format: 16-byte salt + 4-byte big-endian record size + 1-byte keyid-length + 65-byte keyid (your ephemeral public key) as the aes128gcm header, then AES-128-GCM-encrypt(plaintext + 0x02 padding-delimiter byte) appended as the single record.
   - Header on the request: Content-Encoding: aes128gcm (no separate Crypto-Key/Encryption headers — those belonged to the older, deprecated "aesgcm" scheme; aes128gcm folds salt/keyid into the body). Source: rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8291 (has full byte-level spec and a worked test vector in the appendix — plaintext "When I grow up, I want to be a watermelon", exact CEK/nonce/ciphertext bytes — useful to unit-test your implementation against).
   - PONYTAIL CALL: for a single-user personal reminder app, skip step 5 entirely. Send an empty/tickle push (Content-Encoding omitted, no body) and hardcode the notification title/body client-side in the service worker's 'push' handler based on a `tag` or tiny JSON the SW already knows, OR fetch the real text from your API inside the push handler via ctx.waitUntil(fetch(...)). This avoids ~100 lines of ECDH/HKDF/AES-GCM code and its test-vector-matching risk, for a feature (rich push text) a single user doesn't need. Add real encrypted payloads only if you later want the notification body to differ each time (e.g. today's actual weight trend) without the SW making a network round-trip first.

push.workerCron:
Cron Triggers docs: developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/handlers/scheduled/ and limits: developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/

SYNTAX: standard 5-field cron in wrangler.toml:
[triggers]
crons = ["*/15 * * * *"]
Handler:
export default {
  async scheduled(controller, env, ctx) {
    ctx.waitUntil(runReminders(env)); // must be wrapped in waitUntil or the Worker can be killed before async work finishes
  },
};
controller.cron gives you the exact matched cron string (character-for-character) if you register multiple crons and branch with a switch.

FREE TIER LIMITS (confirmed against developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/, contradicts several blog posts that say "5 free" or "3 free" without qualifying per-account vs per-worker):
- 5 Cron Triggers per ACCOUNT on Free (not per Worker) — cronuru/other blogs quoting "3 per worker" are describing an older or different limit; trust the CF docs page over search snippets.
- CPU time per invocation on Free: 10 ms (yes, milliseconds) — this is the sharpest constraint in the whole design. Paid plan gives 30s (<1hr interval crons) or 15 min (>=1hr interval crons).
- Subrequests per invocation on Free: 50. Paid: 10,000 (up to 10M with adjustment).
- Requests/day on Free: 100,000 (scheduled invocations draw from this same daily allowance).
- ALWAYS UTC. No per-trigger timezone setting exists.

FIRING AT A USER'S LOCAL TIME FROM A UTC-ONLY CRON:
Run one Worker on a frequent cron (e.g. every 15 or 30 minutes — a single personal app has plenty of room under the 5-trigger/10ms budget at that cadence) and, inside scheduled(), compute each user's current local hour:minute with Intl.DateTimeFormat, which correctly handles DST without manual offset math:
  const localHM = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-CA', {
    timeZone: user.tz, // IANA string, e.g. 'America/Edmonton' for Calgary (MST/MDT, DST-aware)
    hour: '2-digit', minute: '2-digit', hour12: false,
  }).format(new Date());
Compare localHM against each stored reminder's target hour:minute (rounded to the cron's own granularity, e.g. match within the same 15-min bucket), and send only if it matches AND you haven't already sent that reminder today (dedupe key: `sent:{reminderId}:{YYYY-MM-DD in user's tz}` in KV, checked and set in the same invocation).
Community-documented alternative for going below the 1-minute cron floor or fanning out many schedules from one trigger: aulvem.com/blog/2026-07-25-cloudflare-workers-cron-multiplex/ (collapse many logical schedules into one per-minute cron that branches on computed local time, rather than registering a cron trigger per schedule — relevant here since you're capped at 5 triggers/account anyway).
GOTCHA: at 10ms CPU/invocation on Free, looping over KV lookups + Intl.DateTimeFormat calls + ECDSA JWT signing for more than a handful of users per tick will likely exceed the CPU budget and get the invocation killed mid-way. For a single user this is a non-issue; flag it before this ever becomes multi-tenant.

push.serviceWorkerCode:
// sw.js — network-first for anything that can go stale, cache-first ONLY for
// filenames that change on every build. This is the fix for the black-screen
// bug: a cache-first HTML/entry-JS strategy can freeze the app on a broken
// build forever, because the SW never re-checks the network once something
// is cached, and an old SW can keep controlling the page if skipWaiting/
// clients.claim aren't called. Source pattern confirmed against multiple
// 2026 PWA caching-strategy writeups (borstch.com, javascript.ac, dev.to
// crisiscoresystems) which all converge on: hashed assets = cache-first,
// HTML/entry documents = network-first, cache name = versioned + cleaned
// up in activate.

const BUILD_ID = '__BUILD_ID__'; // replace at build time (git sha / timestamp)
const CACHE_NAME = `app-shell-v${BUILD_ID}`;
const PRECACHE_URLS = ['/', '/manifest.json']; // add hashed bundle paths from your build manifest here

self.addEventListener('install', (event) => {
  self.skipWaiting(); // new SW activates immediately, doesn't wait for old tabs to close
  event.waitUntil(caches.open(CACHE_NAME).then((c) => c.addAll(PRECACHE_URLS)));
});

self.addEventListener('activate', (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches.keys()
      .then((keys) => Promise.all(keys.filter((k) => k !== CACHE_NAME).map((k) => caches.delete(k))))
      .then(() => self.clients.claim()) // take control of already-open tabs now, not on next reload
  );
});

self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
  const { request } = event;
  if (request.method !== 'GET') return;

  const isHashedAsset = /\.[0-9a-f]{8,}\.(js|css|woff2?)$/.test(new URL(request.url).pathname);

  if (isHashedAsset) {
    // Safe to cache-first: the filename itself changes every build, so a
    // cache hit can never be a stale-but-same-name file.
    event.respondWith(
      caches.match(request).then((cached) => cached || fetch(request).then((res) => {
        const copy = res.clone();
        caches.open(CACHE_NAME).then((c) => c.put(request, copy));
        return res;
      }))
    );
    return;
  }

  // Network-first for HTML navigations, unhashed JS/CSS, manifest, everything
  // else: try the network, only fall back to cache if the network truly
  // fails (offline). Never silently serve a cached shell while online, even
  // if the cache has something. This is the line that stops a broken build
  // from being served forever from cache.
  event.respondWith(
    fetch(request)
      .then((res) => {
        const copy = res.clone();
        caches.open(CACHE_NAME).then((c) => c.put(request, copy));
        return res;
      })
      .catch(() => caches.match(request).then((cached) => cached || caches.match('/')))
  );
});

// Push handlers do NOT touch the app-shell cache at all, so a broken/corrupt
// cache can never prevent a notification from firing.
self.addEventListener('push', (event) => {
  let data = {};
  try {
    data = event.data ? event.data.json() : {};
  } catch (e) {
    data = { title: 'Reminder', body: event.data ? event.data.text() : '' };
  }
  const title = data.title || 'Fitness reminder';
  event.waitUntil(
    self.registration.showNotification(title, {
      body: data.body || '',
      icon: '/icon-192.png',
      badge: '/badge-72.png',
      tag: data.tag || 'reminder',
      data: { url: data.url || '/' },
    })
  );
});

self.addEventListener('notificationclick', (event) => {
  event.notification.close();
  const url = event.notification.data && event.notification.data.url || '/';
  event.waitUntil(
    self.clients.matchAll({ type: 'window', includeUncontrolled: true }).then((clients) => {
      const existing = clients.find((c) => c.url.includes(url));
      return existing ? existing.focus() : self.clients.openWindow(url);
    })
  );
});

// ESCAPE HATCH (add this if it ever happens again): a tiny, NEVER-cached
// bootstrap endpoint (e.g. /sw-kill) that the app can fetch on load; if it
// returns a version newer than what this SW thinks it is, call
// registration.unregister() and caches.keys().then(ks => ks.forEach(k =>
// caches.delete(k))) then reload — gives you a remote kill switch without
// requiring the user to manually uninstall the PWA.

push.kvSchema:
One Workers KV namespace is enough at this scale (well under the 1GB/1k-writes-per-day free limits for a single user). Suggested key layout:

push:sub:{userId}
  -> JSON { endpoint, keys: { p256dh, auth }, createdAt, userAgent }
  One per active subscription; overwrite on every successful (re)subscribe. Delete on 404/410 (see gotchas).

push:prefs:{userId}
  -> JSON {
       tz: "America/Edmonton",              // IANA zone for Calgary, DST-aware
       reminders: [
         { id: "meal-log-lunch", hour: 12, minute: 30, enabled: true },
         { id: "meal-log-dinner", hour: 18, minute: 30, enabled: true },
         { id: "weigh-in", hour: 7, minute: 0, enabled: true },
         { id: "gym-session", hour: 17, minute: 0, enabled: true, days: [1,3,5] }, // Mon/Wed/Fri for the 3x/week Crunch Fitness plan
         { id: "retatrutide-dose", hour: 9, minute: 0, enabled: true, days: [0] } // weekly, day-of-week 0=Sunday if that's injection day — confirm actual weekday, only Aug 31/Sep 7/Sep 14 dates were given, not a weekday
       ]
     }

push:sent:{reminderId}:{YYYY-MM-DD}
  -> "1" with a KV expirationTtl of e.g. 172800 (2 days) so it auto-cleans and you never need a delete pass.
  Written once per reminder per local day, checked at the top of scheduled() to dedupe across the multiple cron ticks that land in the same target window.

push:dose-schedule:{userId} (optional, since retatrutide titration dates/doses are known in advance and don't fit a weekly-recurrence model cleanly)
  -> JSON [ { date: "2026-08-31", mg: 1 }, { date: "2026-09-07", mg: 1.5 }, { date: "2026-09-14", mg: 1.5 } ]
  Checked by date match rather than day-of-week, so titration steps fire correctly without recomputing a recurrence rule each time the dose changes.

Note on KV consistency: writes can take up to 60s to propagate to other Cloudflare edge locations (developers.cloudflare.com/kv/concepts/how-kv-works/), and reads of a very-recently-written key from a different location than the write can return stale/absent data during that window. Irrelevant for a single low-frequency personal-reminder writer, but don't build read-your-own-write logic across two near-simultaneous requests without accounting for it.
